PILOT_031,Which condition affecting the coronary arteries should be investigated when assessing narrowed blood supply to heart muscle?,indirect,cardiovascular,coronary artery disease,single_pilot_031,cardiovascular:coronary_artery_disease,concept_resolution,SRC001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Coronary heart disease is the relevant condition. NHLBI explains that atherosclerotic plaque can build up in the coronary arteries on the heart surface, narrowing them and reducing blood flow to the heart muscle.",SRC001,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source verification; STU_001 human sign-off pending. NHLBI > Coronary Heart Disease Causes and Prevention > What causes coronary heart disease?; Plaque buildup. The source directly supports plaque-related coronary narrowing and reduced blood flow, identifying coronary heart disease. Its causes page does not explicitly establish the synonym coronary artery disease; that terminology should be separately confirmed before strict synonym-based gold labeling."
PILOT_032,Which cerebrovascular condition is relevant when a question describes sudden interruption of blood flow to part of the brain?,indirect,cardiovascular,stroke,single_pilot_032,cardiovascular:stroke,concept_resolution,SRC002,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/stroke/causes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Ischemic stroke. This occurs when blood flow to part of the brain is blocked, often by a blood clot or plaque. Stroke can also be hemorrhagic when caused by bleeding; the interruption-by-blockage clue specifically points to ischemic stroke.",SRC002,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source verification; STU_001 human sign-off pending. NHLBI > Stroke Causes and Risk Factors > Causes > Ischemic stroke. NHLBI explicitly describes ischemic stroke as blocked blood flow to the brain and identifies clots or plaque as common obstructions. The answer specifies the subtype rather than applying this mechanism to all strokes.
PILOT_033,Which cardiac condition is being discussed when the heart cannot pump blood effectively enough for the body's needs?,indirect,cardiovascular,heart failure,single_pilot_033,cardiovascular:heart_failure,concept_resolution,SRC003,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart-failure/causes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Heart failure. NHLBI describes heart failure as a condition in which the heart cannot supply sufficient blood to the body. Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction involves impaired pumping, while heart failure with preserved ejection fraction involves inadequate relaxation and filling.",SRC003,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source verification; STU_001 human sign-off pending. NHLBI > Heart Failure Causes and Risk Factors > What causes left-sided heart failure? > HFrEF and HFpEF. The source distinguishes inadequate pumping in HFrEF and impaired filling in HFpEF. This supports the condition identification but does not diagnose a specific patient or subtype.
PILOT_034,Which metabolic disorder should be considered when discussing impaired insulin action and persistent high blood glucose?,indirect,metabolic,type 2 diabetes,single_pilot_034,metabolic:type_2_diabetes,concept_resolution,SRC004,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source verification; STU_001 human sign-off pending. NIDDK > Risk Factors for Type 2 Diabetes > What are the risk factors for type 2 diabetes? The registered page supports risk factors, including obesity, inactivity, and family history, but does not adequately define the insulin-resistance mechanism and persistent hyperglycemia described in the question. Add a registered type 2 diabetes definition/mechanism source before approving a gold answer."
PILOT_035,Which health condition is relevant to a question about excess body fat and long-term metabolic risk?,indirect,metabolic,obesity,single_pilot_035,metabolic:obesity,concept_resolution,SRC005,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/weight-management/adult-overweight-obesity/factors-affecting-weight-health,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source verification; STU_001 human sign-off pending. NIDDK > Factors Affecting Weight & Health > Lifestyle habits; Health problems; Family history and genes. The page discusses weight gain and contributing factors, but it does not establish a full definition of obesity as excess body fat or adequately substantiate the metabolic-risk portion of this question. Register a condition-definition and health-risk source before approving an answer."
PILOT_036,Which respiratory condition involves variable airway narrowing and recurring episodes of wheeze?,indirect,respiratory,asthma,single_pilot_036,respiratory:asthma,concept_resolution,EXT001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/asthma/causes,false,"Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned. Use asthma onset/risk criteria, not triggers of an existing asthma attack.",needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source verification; STU_001 human sign-off pending. NHLBI > Asthma Causes and Triggers > How asthma affects the lungs. The attached row registers EXT001 (NHLBI), not SRC006 (CDC). NHLBI discusses airway inflammation and narrowing, but the current registered source and review workflow do not establish the complete recurrent-wheeze disease definition as a verified concept-resolution answer. Leave the reference answer and supporting IDs blank pending a dedicated asthma overview/definition source. Do not conflate asthma onset with attack triggers."
PILOT_037,Which chronic lung condition is discussed when airflow limitation persists over time?,indirect,respiratory,chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,single_pilot_037,respiratory:chronic_obstructive_pulmonary_disease,concept_resolution,SRC007,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/copd/causes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a long-term lung condition involving persistent airflow limitation. NHLBI identifies cigarette smoking as a leading cause and notes that inhaled irritants can damage airways.,SRC007,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI COPD Causes and Risk Factors identifies the chronic condition and relevant airway injury; it does not itself diagnose a patient.
PILOT_038,Which kidney condition is characterized by long-lasting reduction in kidney function?,indirect,renal,chronic kidney disease,single_pilot_038,renal:chronic_kidney_disease,concept_resolution,SRC008,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease-ckd/causes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is the relevant condition. NIDDK identifies diabetes and high blood pressure among major causes of long-term kidney damage in adults. A person’s CKD diagnosis and stage require clinical evidence.,SRC008,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK Causes of Chronic Kidney Disease in Adults supports the disease identity and major causes; diagnostic duration and staging are not established by this causes page.
PILOT_039,Which neurological disorder is relevant to recurrent headaches with light sensitivity in some patients?,indirect,neurological,migraine,single_pilot_039,neurological:migraine,concept_resolution,SRC009,https://medlineplus.gov/migraine.html,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Migraine is a neurological disorder characterized by recurrent headache attacks that may include sensitivity to light, nausea, or other symptoms. Light sensitivity alone does not establish a diagnosis.",SRC009,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Migraine summary describes recurring attacks and light sensitivity, but symptoms are not diagnostic by themselves."
PILOT_040,Which neurological condition is defined by a tendency toward recurrent unprovoked seizures?,indirect,neurological,epilepsy,single_pilot_040,neurological:epilepsy,concept_resolution,SRC010,https://medlineplus.gov/epilepsy.html,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Epilepsy is a neurological condition characterized by a tendency to have recurrent seizures. It has multiple possible causes, including brain injury, and some cases have no identifiable cause; a single event does not establish epilepsy.",SRC010,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Epilepsy summary supports recurrent seizures and multiple causes; the unprovoked nuance requires diagnostic assessment.
PILOT_041,"Which infectious disease is associated with Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and how might it affect the lungs?",indirect,infectious_disease,tuberculosis,single_pilot_041,infectious_disease:tuberculosis,concept_resolution,EXT004;SRC011,https://www.cdc.gov/tb/causes/index.html;https://www.cdc.gov/tb/risk-factors/index.html,false,"Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned. The named infectious agent identifies TB, but confirm the path to pulmonary disease separately.",answerable,"Tuberculosis (TB) is an infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It often affects the lungs, where active pulmonary TB can cause cough and other respiratory symptoms, but infection may also be latent or involve other organs.",EXT004,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. CDC Causes and How It Spreads identifies M. tuberculosis, airborne transmission and lung involvement; SRC011 concerns risk rather than organism identification."
PILOT_042,Which respiratory infection is relevant when lung air sacs become inflamed and may contain fluid?,indirect,infectious_disease,pneumonia,single_pilot_042,infectious_disease:pneumonia,concept_resolution,SRC012,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/pneumonia/causes,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Pneumonia is a lung infection in which the air sacs can become inflamed and fill with fluid or pus. Bacteria and viruses, including pneumococcus and influenza virus, can cause different types of pneumonia.",SRC012,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI Pneumonia Causes and Risk Factors identifies infectious causes; the air-sac description is background context, not organism-specific identification."
PILOT_043,"Which mental health condition may involve persistent low mood and reduced interest, and what evidence is relevant to its origins?",indirect,mental_health,depression,single_pilot_043,mental_health:depression,concept_resolution,SRC013,https://medlineplus.gov/depression.html,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,"Depression is a mood disorder that can involve persistent low mood and loss of interest. MedlinePlus describes multiple contributing factors, including family history and stressful or traumatic life events. These factors do not independently establish a diagnosis.",SRC013,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Depression includes condition description and multifactorial risk; no factor guarantees an individual diagnosis.
PILOT_044,"Which malignancy originates in lung tissue, and what established exposures should be considered?",indirect,oncology,lung cancer,single_pilot_044,oncology:lung_cancer,concept_resolution,SRC014,https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/patient/lung-prevention-pdq,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,Lung cancer is cancer arising in lung tissue. NCI identifies tobacco smoking as a major risk factor and discusses exposures such as secondhand smoke and radon. Such exposures increase risk but do not determine individual cancer status.,SRC014,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NCI Lung Cancer Prevention identifies disease and population-level risk exposures, not patient diagnosis or precise personal probability."
PILOT_045,Which thyroid disorder is relevant when thyroid hormone production is lower than needed?,indirect,endocrine,hypothyroidism,single_pilot_045,endocrine:hypothyroidism,concept_resolution,SRC015,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/hypothyroidism,false,Candidate topic pages must be checked for correct disease definitions and synonyms; no concept-resolution gold assigned.,answerable,Hypothyroidism is a disorder in which the thyroid gland produces too little thyroid hormone. NIDDK identifies Hashimoto disease and thyroid removal among possible causes.,SRC015,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK Hypothyroidism defines underactive thyroid and documents autoimmune and surgical etiologies.
PILOT_046,"What evidence, if any, supports a pathway from smoking through atherosclerotic plaque development to coronary artery disease?",multi_hop,cardiovascular,coronary artery disease,single_pilot_046,cardiovascular:coronary_artery_disease,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Smoking→plaque→coronary disease requires separate evidentiary checks for both arrows.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC001 establishes coronary plaque as a leading cause of coronary disease, but smoking-to-plaque is not independently established as a specific link by the registered page. Need a smoking/atherosclerosis mechanism source for the two-edge chain."
PILOT_047,"How might persistent high blood pressure affect vascular injury and subsequent stroke risk, and which steps are evidence-supported?",multi_hop,cardiovascular,stroke,single_pilot_047,cardiovascular:stroke,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC002,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/stroke/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Do not treat elevated stroke risk as proof of an exact vascular-injury pathway.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC002 provides stroke causes and risk factors; it is insufficient to validate each proposed step high blood pressure -> vascular injury -> stroke. Obtain mechanism-specific evidence for vascular injury.
PILOT_048,What is the evidence for an indirect pathway from coronary artery disease through heart muscle injury to heart failure?,multi_hop,cardiovascular,heart failure,single_pilot_048,cardiovascular:heart_failure,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC001;SRC003,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart-failure/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. The heart failure subtype and mechanism must be defined before a gold path can be annotated.,answerable,"Coronary heart disease can reduce blood supply to heart muscle, sometimes leading to muscle injury. NHLBI also identifies coronary heart disease or previous heart attack among contributors to heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF). This is a possible pathway, not an inevitable outcome or a universal mechanism for all heart failure.",SRC001;SRC003,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI coronary causes describes reduced coronary blood supply; NHLBI heart-failure causes links coronary disease or infarction with myocardial damage and HFrEF. Subtype qualification retained.
PILOT_049,"Does evidence support a pathway from excess adiposity through insulin resistance to type 2 diabetes, and what are its limitations?",multi_hop,metabolic,type 2 diabetes,single_pilot_049,metabolic:type_2_diabetes,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC004;SRC005,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes;https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/weight-management/adult-overweight-obesity/factors-affecting-weight-health,true,"Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Obesity and insulin resistance may relate to risk, but a particular two-step biological causal chain needs stronger sources.",needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC004 establishes overweight or obesity as a risk factor for type 2 diabetes and SRC005 discusses weight gain, but neither validates both specific arrows excess adiposity -> insulin resistance -> type 2 diabetes. Require insulin-resistance mechanism source."
PILOT_050,"How can chronic high blood glucose contribute to vascular changes and kidney complications, according to evidence?",multi_hop,metabolic,type 2 diabetes,single_pilot_050,metabolic:type_2_diabetes,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC004;SRC008,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes;https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease-ckd/causes,true,"Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. The question refers to kidney complications, which are different from onset of type 2 diabetes.",answerable,"NIDDK describes diabetes-related high blood glucose as damaging kidney filtering structures over time, which can contribute to chronic kidney disease. The registered evidence supports long-term kidney damage, but does not establish every proposed vascular intermediate or the severity for a specific patient.",SRC008,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC008 directly supports high blood glucose damaging kidneys in adults with diabetes. SRC004 concerns risk of developing diabetes and is not needed for the kidney-complication answer.
PILOT_051,Can environmental exposure contribute to airway inflammation and asthma symptoms through intermediate mechanisms?,multi_hop,respiratory,asthma,single_pilot_051,respiratory:asthma,multi_hop_link_evidence,EXT001;SRC006,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/asthma/causes;https://www.cdc.gov/asthma/control/index.html,true,"Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Asthma onset, airway inflammation and attack triggers are distinct phenomena.",needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. EXT001 discusses asthma causes and airway inflammation and SRC006 discusses triggers of existing asthma; the complete exposure -> inflammation -> symptom pathway is not established uniformly for each exposure. Need matched mechanistic evidence.
PILOT_052,"How does evidence connect tobacco exposure, chronic airway inflammation, and airflow limitation?",multi_hop,respiratory,chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,single_pilot_052,respiratory:chronic_obstructive_pulmonary_disease,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC007,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/copd/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC007 links chronic tobacco exposure with COPD and airway damage, but is insufficient to validate chronic inflammation as a distinct intermediate in the precise three-node causal path."
PILOT_053,Which intermediate biological processes might connect longstanding high blood pressure with chronic kidney disease?,multi_hop,renal,chronic kidney disease,single_pilot_053,renal:chronic_kidney_disease,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC008,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease-ckd/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Hypertension and kidney disease can influence each other; directionality needs explicit evidence.,answerable,"Longstanding high blood pressure can damage blood vessels in the kidneys, limiting their ability to filter wastes and contributing to chronic kidney disease. Kidney disease can also worsen blood pressure; this is not a one-directional explanation in every patient.",SRC008,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK CKD causes explains hypertension-related damage to renal blood vessels and acknowledges mutual effects; supports the qualified pathway.
PILOT_054,What evidence supports or contradicts a proposed pathway from atrial fibrillation through clot formation to ischemic stroke?,multi_hop,neurological,stroke,single_pilot_054,neurological:stroke,multi_hop_link_evidence,EXT002;SRC002,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/atrial-fibrillation/living-with;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/stroke/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Atrial fibrillation→clot→ischemic stroke needs distinct mechanism support; distinguish stroke subtypes.,answerable,"Atrial fibrillation can allow blood to pool in the heart and form clots; a clot that travels to and blocks a brain artery can cause an ischemic stroke. The pathway is a possible complication, not proof of stroke in every patient with atrial fibrillation.",EXT002;SRC002,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI atrial fibrillation material connects AF with clot and stroke risk, while the stroke causes page establishes clot obstruction in ischemic stroke; avoid hemorrhagic-stroke conflation."
PILOT_055,"Could prior brain injury lead to structural changes and later seizure risk, and which links are supported?",multi_hop,neurological,epilepsy,single_pilot_055,neurological:epilepsy,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC010,https://medlineplus.gov/epilepsy.html,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. An epilepsy risk factor is not enough to prove an intermediate epileptogenic pathway.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Epilepsy includes brain injury as a possible cause but does not explain the specified intermediate structural changes or demonstrate the exact path to recurrent seizures.
PILOT_056,What evidence describes a possible pathway from tuberculosis infection through lung damage to persistent breathing limitation?,multi_hop,infectious_disease,tuberculosis,single_pilot_056,infectious_disease:tuberculosis,multi_hop_link_evidence,EXT004;SRC011,https://www.cdc.gov/tb/causes/index.html;https://www.cdc.gov/tb/risk-factors/index.html,true,"Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. TB transmission, tissue injury and subsequent pulmonary impairment require separate evidence.",needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. CDC pages establish TB infection and some risks, but they do not substantiate a full progression from lung tissue injury to persistent post-TB breathing limitation. Add post-TB lung disease evidence."
PILOT_057,Which intermediate immune responses might connect respiratory infection with the clinical manifestations of pneumonia?,multi_hop,infectious_disease,pneumonia,single_pilot_057,infectious_disease:pneumonia,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC012,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/pneumonia/causes,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Immune response steps need mechanism-specific sources; a general pneumonia overview is insufficient.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC012 identifies bacterial and viral pneumonia causes but does not supply a sufficiently specified immune-response cascade linking infection to manifestations.
PILOT_058,"What evidence exists for pathways linking chronic stress, sleep disruption, and depressive symptoms without assuming causality?",multi_hop,mental_health,depression,single_pilot_058,mental_health:depression,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC013,https://medlineplus.gov/depression.html,true,"Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Stress, sleep and depression associations must not be promoted to an unsupported causal chain.",needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Depression documents stress-related risk and sleep-related symptoms but cannot verify a specific direction of the stress -> sleep disruption -> depression chain; observational association must not be made causal.
PILOT_059,How might prolonged exposure to tobacco smoke relate to cellular DNA damage and lung cancer development?,multi_hop,oncology,lung cancer,single_pilot_059,oncology:lung_cancer,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC014,https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/patient/lung-prevention-pdq,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Carcinogenesis mechanisms require more than a lung-cancer prevention risk summary.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NCI prevention source establishes tobacco smoke as a lung cancer risk factor; an explicit two-link cellular DNA damage and carcinogenesis pathway requires an appropriately detailed mechanism source.
PILOT_060,Can autoimmune thyroid inflammation lead to reduced thyroid hormone production through intermediate tissue changes?,multi_hop,endocrine,hypothyroidism,single_pilot_060,endocrine:hypothyroidism,multi_hop_link_evidence,SRC015,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/hypothyroidism,true,Each proposed causal arrow needs independent support; topic-level sources are not verified multi-hop evidence. Hashimoto thyroid injury→hormone deficit needs mechanism-specific review.,answerable,"Hashimoto disease involves the immune system attacking and damaging the thyroid gland, which can reduce thyroid-hormone production and result in hypothyroidism. This is an established mechanism but not the only cause of hypothyroidism.",SRC015,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK Hypothyroidism describes autoimmune damage to thyroid and resulting hormone deficiency under Hashimoto disease.
PILOT_061,How do established risk factors for stroke compare with those for coronary artery disease?,comparative,cardiovascular,stroke,single_pilot_061,cardiovascular:stroke,two_topic_comparison,SRC002;SRC001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/stroke/causes;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence.,answerable,"Both stroke and coronary heart disease involve vascular disease and share important risks such as high blood pressure and smoking. Coronary disease most commonly involves plaque affecting coronary arteries; ischemic stroke results from interrupted blood supply to brain tissue, whereas hemorrhagic stroke involves bleeding. Shared risk does not mean identical mechanisms.",SRC002;SRC001,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. Both NHLBI pages provide vascular causes and overlapping risks; stroke subtypes must remain separate.
PILOT_062,How do the mechanisms implicated in heart failure differ from those associated with coronary artery disease?,comparative,cardiovascular,heart failure,single_pilot_062,cardiovascular:heart_failure,two_topic_comparison,SRC003;SRC001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart-failure/causes;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence.,answerable,"Coronary heart disease commonly develops when plaque limits blood flow through coronary arteries. Heart failure describes inadequate heart pumping or filling; coronary disease or a prior heart attack may contribute to HFrEF, while HFpEF can arise with long-term pressure-related stiffness. They are related but distinct conditions.",SRC003;SRC001,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. The paired NHLBI pages describe the coronary narrowing mechanism and HFrEF/HFpEF heart-failure mechanisms.
PILOT_063,How does the evidence on obesity-related risk differ between type 2 diabetes and coronary artery disease?,comparative,metabolic,type 2 diabetes,single_pilot_063,metabolic:type_2_diabetes,two_topic_comparison,SRC004;SRC001,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. SRC004 explicitly links obesity with increased type 2 diabetes risk, while SRC001 focuses on coronary disease mechanisms and does not provide a comparable obesity-related risk assessment. Need coronary risk-factor source for two-sided comparison."
PILOT_064,"Which associations are shared by obesity and metabolic syndrome, and which should not be interpreted as causal?",comparative,metabolic,obesity,single_pilot_064,metabolic:obesity,two_topic_comparison,SRC005;EXT005,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/weight-management/adult-overweight-obesity/factors-affecting-weight-health;https://medlineplus.gov/metabolicsyndrome.html,false,"Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of risk factors, not synonymous with obesity.",answerable,"Obesity and metabolic syndrome can overlap, particularly through excess abdominal fat and metabolic abnormalities. Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of risk factors such as elevated blood pressure, abnormal glucose, and abnormal lipids; obesity is not the same diagnosis, and shared associations are not proof of a direct causal pathway.",SRC005;EXT005,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus defines metabolic syndrome as a risk-factor cluster; NIDDK describes weight-related influences. Association is not equivalence or individual causation.
PILOT_065,How do asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease differ in their common triggers and underlying mechanisms?,comparative,respiratory,asthma,single_pilot_065,respiratory:asthma,two_topic_comparison,EXT001;SRC007,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/asthma/causes;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/copd/causes,false,"Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. Use asthma onset/risk sources, not only attack-trigger management.",answerable,"Asthma commonly involves variable airway narrowing and symptoms that can flare with triggers such as allergens or irritants. COPD usually involves persistent airflow limitation associated with chronic injury from smoking or other inhaled irritants. Some exposures overlap, but mechanisms and patterns differ and neither source alone diagnoses an individual.",EXT001;SRC007,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI asthma causes/triggers and COPD causes/risk factors support a qualified comparison, not a diagnostic distinction for a particular person."
PILOT_066,How do the causes and contributing factors of pneumonia differ from those of asthma?,comparative,respiratory,pneumonia,single_pilot_066,respiratory:pneumonia,two_topic_comparison,SRC012;EXT001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/pneumonia/causes;https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/asthma/causes,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence.,answerable,"Pneumonia is typically an infection caused by bacteria, viruses or other pathogens affecting the lungs. Asthma is a chronic airway disorder with inflammation and variable narrowing; triggers can worsen asthma symptoms, but these are not the same as infectious causes of pneumonia.",SRC012;EXT001,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI pneumonia and asthma pages directly distinguish infectious etiology from asthma disease and exacerbation triggers.
PILOT_067,How does the relationship between diabetes and chronic kidney disease compare with that between hypertension and kidney disease?,comparative,renal,chronic kidney disease,single_pilot_067,renal:chronic_kidney_disease,two_topic_comparison,SRC008;SRC004,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease-ckd/causes;https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence.,answerable,"Both diabetes and high blood pressure are common causes of chronic kidney disease in adults. Persistent high blood glucose can damage the kidney filters; high blood pressure can injure renal blood vessels and filtering structures. Kidney disease can in turn worsen blood pressure, so directionality matters.",SRC008,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK CKD causes provides both causal mechanisms directly. SRC004 only describes diabetes onset risks and is not needed for these renal mechanisms.
PILOT_068,How do common migraine triggers differ from established causes of secondary headaches?,comparative,neurological,migraine,single_pilot_068,neurological:migraine,two_topic_comparison,SRC009;EXT006,https://medlineplus.gov/migraine.html;https://medlineplus.gov/headache.html,false,"Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. Compare migraine triggers against causes of secondary headaches, not all headaches.",answerable,"Migraine triggers, such as particular environmental or lifestyle factors, can provoke attacks in people with migraine but are not necessarily the underlying cause of the disorder. Secondary headaches arise because of another condition, such as infection or injury. A trigger and an underlying disease cause should not be treated as equivalent.",SRC009;EXT006,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Migraine and Headache distinguish migraine attacks/triggers from headaches secondary to other conditions; do not claim all headaches are secondary.
PILOT_069,How do factors associated with epilepsy differ from triggers of an individual seizure?,comparative,neurological,epilepsy,single_pilot_069,neurological:epilepsy,two_topic_comparison,SRC010,https://medlineplus.gov/epilepsy.html,true,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. A single epilepsy summary may not distinguish epileptic condition risks from one-off seizure triggers.,needs_more_evidence,,,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Epilepsy outlines epilepsy and possible underlying causes but does not adequately distinguish the range of immediate seizure precipitants from factors that lead to the chronic epilepsy diagnosis. Require seizure trigger source.
PILOT_070,How do transmission mechanisms of tuberculosis compare with those of influenza?,comparative,infectious_disease,tuberculosis,single_pilot_070,infectious_disease:tuberculosis,two_topic_comparison,EXT004;EXT003,https://www.cdc.gov/tb/causes/index.html;https://www.cdc.gov/flu/spread/,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. TB is airborne and influenza has respiratory transmission; definitions and epidemiological context differ.,answerable,"Tuberculosis is caused by M. tuberculosis and generally spreads through airborne particles released by someone with infectious pulmonary or laryngeal TB. Influenza spreads mainly through respiratory particles produced when infected people cough, sneeze or talk; the infectious organisms and transmission contexts differ. Both can spread through the air, but their transmission patterns should not be treated as identical.",EXT004;EXT003,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. CDC TB causes and CDC flu spread describe each pathogen and modes of respiratory spread. Avoid inventing numeric transmission comparisons.
PILOT_071,How do risk factors for depression compare with those for anxiety disorders?,comparative,mental_health,depression,single_pilot_071,mental_health:depression,two_topic_comparison,SRC013;EXT007,https://medlineplus.gov/depression.html;https://medlineplus.gov/anxiety.html,false,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. Depression and anxiety disorders have distinct diagnostic criteria and heterogeneous risks.,answerable,"Depression and anxiety disorders can both be associated with family history and stressful life experiences. Depression is typically characterized by persistent low mood or loss of interest, while anxiety disorders involve excessive anxiety or fear. These associations overlap but the conditions have distinct presentations and are not explained by one universal cause.",SRC013;EXT007,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Depression and Anxiety summarize risk and typical symptoms; comparison is qualitative, not proof of equal causal effects."
PILOT_072,How do established lung cancer risk factors compare between people who smoke and people who have never smoked?,comparative,oncology,lung cancer,single_pilot_072,oncology:lung_cancer,two_topic_comparison,SRC014,https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/patient/lung-prevention-pdq,true,Reviewer must verify each side of the comparison; overlapping risk factors do not prove causal equivalence. A single general cancer-risk page may not fully support smoker/non-smoker risk stratification.,answerable,"Tobacco smoking is the major established lung cancer risk factor and substantially elevates risk among smokers. People who have never smoked can still develop lung cancer, with risks that include radon and secondhand smoke. A general risk-factor listing cannot assign precise individual probabilities or comparative magnitudes for every exposure.",SRC014,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NCI lung cancer prevention names smoking, secondhand smoke and radon risk, but risk estimates for never-smokers should not be inferred without stratified evidence."
PILOT_073,"Which modifiable risk factors for stroke have strong evidence, and which are only associated with risk?",risk_factor,cardiovascular,stroke,single_pilot_073,cardiovascular:stroke,population_risk,SRC002,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/stroke/causes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"NHLBI reports modifiable stroke risk factors such as high blood pressure, smoking and diabetes-related vascular risk. These increase the probability of stroke; a risk-factor listing does not by itself establish the magnitude or causal certainty of every association, nor predict an individual stroke.",SRC002,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,"AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. Stroke Causes and Risk Factors identifies modifiable risks, but source does not rank each by comparative causal evidence strength."
PILOT_074,What evidence supports elevated blood pressure as a risk factor for coronary artery disease?,risk_factor,cardiovascular,coronary artery disease,single_pilot_074,cardiovascular:coronary_artery_disease,population_risk,SRC001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/coronary-heart-disease/causes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,Long-term high blood pressure can contribute to injury or dysfunction of coronary blood vessels and is a relevant factor in coronary heart disease development. The page does not quantify an individual risk or show that hypertension is the direct cause of every case.,SRC001,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI Coronary Heart Disease Causes and Prevention discusses high blood pressure as a contributor to coronary vessel dysfunction; evidence is contextual rather than a personal risk estimate.
PILOT_075,Which factors increase the likelihood of developing heart failure in adults?,risk_factor,cardiovascular,heart failure,single_pilot_075,cardiovascular:heart_failure,population_risk,SRC003,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart-failure/causes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"Conditions that can increase heart-failure risk include coronary heart disease, past heart attack and high blood pressure. They may damage, overwork or stiffen the heart; reduced- and preserved-ejection-fraction heart failure have different mechanisms.",SRC003,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI Heart Failure Causes and Risk Factors lists conditions and distinguishes HFrEF/HFpEF; not an exhaustive adult risk score.
PILOT_076,Which lifestyle and inherited factors are associated with future type 2 diabetes risk?,risk_factor,metabolic,type 2 diabetes,single_pilot_076,metabolic:type_2_diabetes,population_risk,SRC004,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"NIDDK lists overweight or obesity, physical inactivity, family history of diabetes, older age, prediabetes and a history of gestational diabetes among type 2 diabetes risk factors. Lifestyle and inherited or familial characteristics raise probability, but none ensures onset.",SRC004,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK Risk Factors for Type 2 Diabetes directly supports all examples as risk associations rather than deterministic causes.
PILOT_077,Which environmental and behavioral factors are associated with obesity risk at a population level?,risk_factor,metabolic,obesity,single_pilot_077,metabolic:obesity,population_risk,SRC005,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/weight-management/adult-overweight-obesity/factors-affecting-weight-health,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"NIDDK describes eating patterns, physical activity and sleep as behavioral influences on weight. Environmental and social circumstances, medicines, health conditions and genetic factors can also influence weight and obesity risk. Evidence about weight gain is not automatically proof of a direct obesity cause.",SRC005,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK Factors Affecting Weight & Health discusses multiple influences; do not quantify effects or equate all observed weight gain with obesity diagnosis.
PILOT_078,"Which environmental exposures are associated with asthma onset, and how certain is the evidence?",risk_factor,respiratory,asthma,single_pilot_078,respiratory:asthma,population_risk,EXT001,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/asthma/causes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis. Document onset risk, not asthma-attack triggers.",answerable,"NHLBI reports that environmental exposures such as tobacco smoke and air pollution may contribute to asthma risk, although asthma has multiple genetic and environmental influences and its exact causes remain incompletely understood. Triggers of an asthma attack in an existing patient must be distinguished from factors associated with developing asthma.",EXT001,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI Asthma Causes and Triggers discusses environmental and genetic influences while separating onset from acute triggers; certainty is qualified rather than quantified.
PILOT_079,What is known about occupational exposure as a risk factor for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?,risk_factor,respiratory,chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,single_pilot_079,respiratory:chronic_obstructive_pulmonary_disease,population_risk,SRC007,https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/copd/causes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"Long-term workplace exposure to dust, chemical fumes and other lung irritants can raise COPD risk, particularly with prolonged inhalation exposure. Cigarette smoking remains the most common cause in the United States, and occupational exposure does not imply that an individual will develop COPD.",SRC007,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NHLBI COPD Causes and Risk Factors explicitly identifies inhaled occupational irritants as risk factors.
PILOT_080,How do hypertension and diabetes relate to risk of chronic kidney disease?,risk_factor,renal,chronic kidney disease,single_pilot_080,renal:chronic_kidney_disease,population_risk,SRC008;SRC004,https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease-ckd/causes;https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/risk-factors-type-2-diabetes,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,Diabetes and high blood pressure are leading causes of chronic kidney disease in adults. Elevated glucose can damage kidney filters and persistent hypertension can harm renal blood vessels; kidney disease may also aggravate hypertension.,SRC008,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NIDDK CKD causes directly supports renal impacts of hyperglycemia and hypertension; SRC004 pertains to diabetes onset and is not necessary for this answer.
PILOT_081,"Which factors are linked to developing migraine, as distinct from provoking an attack?",risk_factor,neurological,migraine,single_pilot_081,neurological:migraine,population_risk,SRC009,https://medlineplus.gov/migraine.html,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"Family history is associated with greater likelihood of developing migraine, while specific triggers such as stress or changes in sleep can provoke attacks in someone already susceptible. A trigger for an episode is not necessarily a cause of developing the migraine disorder.",SRC009,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Migraine lists familial risk and attack triggers and keeps etiology separate from acute triggering.
PILOT_082,Which conditions increase the likelihood of progression from latent tuberculosis infection to active disease?,risk_factor,infectious_disease,tuberculosis,single_pilot_082,infectious_disease:tuberculosis,population_risk,SRC011;EXT004,https://www.cdc.gov/tb/risk-factors/index.html;https://www.cdc.gov/tb/causes/index.html,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"In someone already infected with TB bacteria, weakened immunity increases the risk of progression to active TB disease. CDC identifies conditions and treatments that weaken the immune system as relevant; exposure and acquisition of infection are distinct from later progression.",SRC011,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. CDC TB Risk Factors distinguishes infection risk from development of active TB among infected people.
PILOT_083,"Which social or biological factors are associated with depression risk, and what causal claims are justified?",risk_factor,mental_health,depression,single_pilot_083,mental_health:depression,population_risk,SRC013,https://medlineplus.gov/depression.html,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"MedlinePlus identifies family history, trauma or stressful life events and other biological or life circumstances as factors associated with higher depression risk. Depression is multifactorial, so these associations do not show that one exposure directly caused a particular individual’s illness.",SRC013,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. MedlinePlus Depression discusses multifactorial causes and risk; no deterministic individual attribution supported.
PILOT_084,How does evidence distinguish established lung cancer risk factors from uncertain associations?,risk_factor,oncology,lung cancer,single_pilot_084,oncology:lung_cancer,population_risk,SRC014,https://www.cancer.gov/types/lung/patient/lung-prevention-pdq,false,"Distinguish population risk association, genuine causation, and individual prognosis.",answerable,"NCI identifies smoking as the major established lung cancer risk factor and discusses other evidence-supported exposures such as radon and secondhand smoke. Evidence varies across exposures, and a risk association does not mean that a particular individual will develop lung cancer.",SRC014,,,,,,,,,,STU_001,2026-11-14,AI-assisted source review; STU_001 human approval pending. NCI prevention evidence separates established risk factors from potential factors; avoid claiming quantified certainty for unspecified associations.
